  {
   "id": "HITl1YfaAQAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2020-05-30T21:14:38Z",
   "subject": "Re: \"Standard Error\" of difference of two effect sizes in lavaan",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I was wondering given <a href=\"https://stats.stackexchange.com/a/258583/140365\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stats.stackexchange.com/a/258583/140365&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421692179000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2T7-DnNFZz-AcjWLsJKMlC\">this answer</a> by Dr. Cheung, how I can calculate the &quot;Standard Error&quot; of my &quot;<b>New.Effect.Size</b>&quot;?</div></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>You can use the delta method, the same way Mike Cheung showed you in that link.  Define your effect size as a parameter in the model syntax, and your <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">summary() </font>output will show your estimated <i>SE</i> for the defined effect size.  You could also use the Monte Carlo method (see the semTools package: <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">?monteCarloMed </font>help page examples).</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421692179000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3kIDbl6hSJgj_05zPPHbcO\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "R3F58mlHAQAJ",
   "from": "Reza Norouzian",
   "author": "8f8b5832b9ad8628",
   "date": "2020-05-30T22:10:50Z",
   "subject": "Re: \"Standard Error\" of difference of two effect sizes in lavaan",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Dr. Jorgensen,<div><br></div><div>Thank you for your insightful comments. To be clear, I want to obtain the &quot;Standard Error&quot; for: <b>SMD.dif.T - \r\n\r\nSMD.dif.C /</b> ( [<b>sqrt(sd_pre.C^2+sd_post.C^2</b><b>-2*sd_pre.C*sd_post.C*r) + </b><b>sqrt(sd_pre.T^2+sd_post.T^2</b><b>-2*sd_pre.T*sd_post.T*r)</b>] <b>/ 2 ) </b>as defined in my 2 effect size models below.</div><div><br></div><div>Would you please assist me in demonstrating this?</div><div><br></div><div>Many thanks, Reza</div><div><br><div><b style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">#--------- Effect size for Control group (</b><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">everything ends in &quot;.C&quot;</span><b style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">):</b></div><div><font face=\"monospace\">library(&quot;lavaan&quot;)<br>## Sample correlation matrix between pre- and post-test scores<br>lower.C &lt;- &#39;1<br>           .5 1&#39;<br>( Cov.C &lt;- getCov(lower, diag = TRUE, names = c(&quot;x_pre.C&quot;,&quot;x_post.C&quot;)) )<br><br>## Sample means for the pre- and post-test scores<br>Mean.C &lt;- c(10, 13)<br><br>## Sample size<br>N.C &lt;- 50<br><br><b>model.C </b>&lt;- &#39;# Label the sds with sd_pre and sd_post<br>eta_pre.C = ~ sd_pre.C*x_pre.C<br>eta_post.C = ~ sd_post.C*x_post.C<br><br># r: correlation betwen pre- and post-test<br>eta_pre.C ~ r*eta_post.C<br><br># Fix the error variances at 0<br>x_pre.C ~ 0*x_pre.C<br>x_post.C ~ 0*x_post.C<br><br># Label the means with m_pre and m_post<br>x_pre.C ~ m_pre.C*1<br>x_post.C ~ m_post.C*1<br><br># Calculate the effect sizes using sd of difference score as the standardizer:<br><b><span style=\"background-color:rgb(0,255,255)\">SMD.dif.C</span> := (m_post.C - m_pre.C) / sqrt(sd_pre.C^2+sd_post.C^2</b><b>-2*sd_pre.C*sd_post.C*r) </b>&#39;<br><br>fit.C &lt;- cfa(model.C, sample.cov = Cov.C, sample.mean = Mean.C,<br>            sample.nobs= N.C, <a href=\"http://std.lv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://std.lv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421692181000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0U-IiBYhFMdQjnS0lR06zg\">std.lv</a> = TRUE,<br>            sample.cov.rescale = FALSE)</font><br></div><div><font face=\"monospace\"><br></font></div><div><b><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">#--------- Effect size for Treatment group (</span></b><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,0)\">everything ends in &quot;.T&quot;<b>):</b></span></div><div><font face=\"monospace\">lower.T &lt;- &#39;1<br>           .6 1&#39;<br>( Cov.T &lt;- getCov(lower, diag = TRUE, names = c(&quot;x_pre.T&quot;,&quot;x_post.T&quot;)) )<br><br>## Sample means for the pre- and post-test scores<br>Mean.T &lt;- c(12, 16)<br><br>## Sample size<br>N.T &lt;- 39<br><br><b>model.T</b> &lt;- &#39;# Label the sds with sd_pre and sd_post<br>eta_pre.T = ~ sd_pre.T*x_pre.T<br>eta_post.T = ~ sd_post.T*x_post.T<br><br># r: correlation betwen pre- and post-test<br>eta_pre.T ~ r*eta_post.T<br><br># Fix the error variances at 0<br>x_pre.T ~ 0*x_pre.T<br>x_post.T ~ 0*x_post.T<br><br># Label the means with m_pre and m_post<br>x_pre.T ~ m_pre.T*1<br>x_post.T ~ m_post.T*1<br><br># Calculate the effect sizes using sd of difference score as the standardizer:<br><b><span style=\"background-color:rgb(0,255,255)\">SMD.dif.T</span> := (m_post.T - m_pre.T) / sqrt(sd_pre.T^2+sd_post.T^2 </b><b>-2*sd_pre.T*sd_post.T*r)</b> &#39;<br><br>fit.T &lt;- cfa(model.T, sample.cov = Cov.T, sample.mean = Mean.T,<br>             sample.nobs= N.T, <a href=\"http://std.lv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://std.lv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421692181000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0U-IiBYhFMdQjnS0lR06zg\">std.lv</a> = TRUE,<br>             sample.cov.rescale = FALSE)<br></font></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sat, May 30, 2020 at 4:14 PM Terrence Jorgensen &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tjorge...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I was wondering given <a href=\"https://stats.stackexchange.com/a/258583/140365\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stats.stackexchange.com/a/258583/140365&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421692181000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1ALDKN9ubZXWvh1okQ9tXR\">this answer</a> by Dr. Cheung, how I can calculate the &quot;Standard Error&quot; of my &quot;<b>New.Effect.Size</b>&quot;?</div></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>You can use the delta method, the same way Mike Cheung showed you in that link.  Define your effect size as a parameter in the model syntax, and your <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">summary() </font>output will show your estimated <i>SE</i> for the defined effect size.  You could also use the Monte Carlo method (see the semTools package: <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">?monteCarloMed </font>help page examples).</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421692181000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3xQxjEFDZAt9nE6YKpvpfh\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;lavaan&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">lavaan+un...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/lavaan/2359f700-ea72-4a92-b5ed-801e851cad13%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/lavaan/2359f700-ea72-4a92-b5ed-801e851cad13%2540googlegroups.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421692181000&amp;usg=AOvVaw33UyP9LbwZBluYCSQ_ZAEm\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/lavaan/2359f700-ea72-4a92-b5ed-801e851cad13%40googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div>"
